{{Listen
| type         = music
| filename     = Love Story.ogg
| title        = "Love Story"
| description  = A sample of the song's [[bridge (music)|bridge]] and a [[key change]] to the final [[refrain]], featuring the accelerated drums and harmonization of melody and vocals over acoustic strings
}}

"Love Story" is a [[midtempo]] [[country pop]] song,<ref name="Bream">{{cite news|title=Download This|last=Bream|first=Jon|newspaper=[[Star Tribune]]|date=October 5, 2008|page=E2|id={{ProQuest|428001558}}}}</ref><ref name="Billboard-top">{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/lists/top-country-love-songs-of-all-time/taylor-swift-love-story/|title=Top 50 Country Love Songs of All Time|last1=Dauphin|first1=Chuck|last2=Pascual|first2=Danielle|date=July 1, 2022|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|access-date=August 26, 2022|archive-date=December 9, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221209060702/https://www.billboard.com/lists/top-country-love-songs-of-all-time/taylor-swift-love-story|url-status=live}}</ref> with a [[tempo]] of 120 beats per minute and a [[4/4 time|{{music|time|4|4}} time]], set in the [[Key (music)|key]] of [[D major]].{{Sfn|Somville|Benoit|2025|p=68}} Its arrangement is driven by acoustic instruments including banjo, fiddle, mandolin, and guitar.<ref name="He-2021"/> Each refrain is accentuated by fiddle and layered electric guitar, drenched in [[Reverb effect|reverb]] and [[Delay (audio effect)|delay]] effects.{{Sfn|Somville|Benoit|2025|p=69}} Jon Bream from the ''[[Star Tribune]]'' described the single as "pure pop with a minimalist vibe" that suits both country and pop radio.<ref name="Bream"/> According to ''[[The New York Times]]'', despite the banjo and fiddle, the song could "easily be an emo rocker".<ref>{{cite news|title=C.M.A. Again Picks Chesney as Entertainer of the Year|newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|date=November 12, 2008|id={{ProQuest|2221241354}}}}</ref> Swift's vocals have a slight [[twang]].<ref name="AV">{{cite web|url=https://www.avclub.com/taylor-swift-just-unleashed-the-full-taylors-version-1846256192|title=Taylor Swift Just Unleashed the Full 'Taylor's Version' of 2008's 'Love Story'|first=William|last=Hughes|date=February 12, 2021|website=[[The A.V. Club]]|access-date=August 26, 2022|archive-date=December 9, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221209060657/https://www.avclub.com/taylor-swift-just-unleashed-the-full-taylors-version-1846256192|url-status=live}}</ref> The mix and master, according to ''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]''{{'s}} Kristen He, are loud and "dynamically flat&nbsp;... [and are] designed to burst out of FM radio speakers".<ref name="He-2021"/>

The lyrics of "Love Story" narrate a troubled romance between two characters, drawing from the lead characters in Shakespeare's ''Romeo and Juliet''.<ref name="bbc">{{cite web |last=McAlpine |first=Fraser |date=February 28, 2009 |title=Taylor Swift – 'Love Story' |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/blogs/chartblog/2009/02/taylor_swift_love_story.shtml |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101107085117/http://www.bbc.co.uk/blogs/chartblog/2009/02/taylor_swift_love_story.shtml |archive-date=November 7, 2010 |access-date=March 8, 2011 |publisher=[[BBC]]}}</ref> According to the psychologist Katie Barclay, the song explores feelings of love in the contexts of pain and joy.{{sfn|Barclay|2018|p=546}} "Love Story", save for the final refrain, is narrated from Juliet's perspective.{{sfn|Tuan|2020|p=27}}<ref name="boston" /> In the [[verse (popular music)|verses]], Juliet tells the story of hers and Romeo's challenged courtship, of which her father disapproves.{{sfnm|1a1=Perone|1y=2017|1p=21|2a1=Barclay|2y=2018|2p=546}} The first verse introduces Juliet in a scene, "We were both young when I first saw you / I close my eyes and the flashback starts, I'm standing there / On a balcony in summer air", which references the balcony scene in Act II, scene ii of Shakespeare's play.{{sfn|Tuan|2020|p=27}} In the refrains, which alter slightly as the song progresses to accompany the narrative, Juliet pleads for her love interest to appear, "Romeo, take me somewhere we can be alone / I'll be waiting / All there's left to do is run."{{sfn|Barclay|2018|p=546}}<ref name="boston" />

In the second verse, Juliet meets Romeo again in a garden and learns he must leave town because of her father's disapproval.{{sfn|Spencer|2010|p=62}} Their relationship encounters difficulties, {{"-}}'Cause you were Romeo, I was a scarlet letter", referencing [[Nathaniel Hawthorne]]'s ''[[The Scarlet Letter]]'' (1850).{{sfn|Tuan|2020|p=28}} According to the media-and-film scholar Iris H. Tuan, Hawthrone's "scarlet letter" imagery represents the female protagonist [[Hester Prynne]]'s sin and adultery, whereas Swift's use symbolizes the forbidden love between Romeo and Juliet.{{sfn|Tuan|2020|p=28}} Juliet pleads, "This love is difficult, but it's real", which Swift said was her favorite lyric in the song.{{sfnm|1a1=Barclay|1y=2018|1p=546|2a1=Spencer|2y=2010|2p=62}}

After the [[bridge (music)|bridge]], with accelerated drums and the [[harmonization]] of melody and vocals, the final refrain incorporates a [[key change]] up a [[whole step]], from D major to [[E major]].{{sfnm|1a1=Sloan|1a2=Harding|1a3=Gottlieb|1y=2019|1pp=34–35|2a1=Barclay|2y=2018|2p=547|3a1=Somville|3a2=Benoit|3y=2025|3pp=68–69}} The final refrain is narrated from Romeo's perspective and tells of his marriage proposal to Juliet after he has sought her father's approval, "I talked to your dad, go pick out a white dress."{{sfnm|1a1=Sloan |1y=2021|1p=15|2a1=Barclay |2y=2018|2p=547|3a1=Tuan|3y=2020|3p=28}} Whereas Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet are secretly married without their parents' approval and both commit suicide, the characters in "Love Story" depart from that ending.{{sfnm|1a1=Perone|1y=2017|1p=21|2a1=Tuan|2y=2020|2p=28|3a1=Spencer|3y=2010|3p=65}} According to Tuan, by projecting her feelings and fantasy on a ''Romeo and Juliet''-inspired narrative, Swift created a song that strongly resonates with an audience of teenage girls and young women.{{sfn|Tuan|2020|pp=30–31}} Deborah Evans Price of ''Billboard'' agreed but also said "one doesn't have to be a lovestruck teen" to enjoy the song's emotional engagement.<ref name="Price"/>

